<SddRecoverFromCode keywords="recover, from-code, restore-spec, path, brownfield, existing-scope, scope-fit, module-split, incremental, lazy, inventory-reverse, sdd-recover-from-code" type="directive" ver="1.1">
  <Mission>
    Recover a spec for ONE named path — a folder or a file the operator points at — from the code
    that already lives there plus whatever sits next to it (README, ADR, old docs, tests). Entered
    from the router when the portal is LIVE and the operator both asks to recover / recreate a spec
    from code AND names a concrete path; the router-core beliefs it relies on are declared in
    `BeliefState deps` (inherited, not repeated).

    Distinct from the two neighboring recovery flows:
    - `discover-from-code` recovers the WHOLE project's general picture and only enters from `root`
      when the portal is absent. This flow is the opposite scale: one path, portal already live.
    - `reconcile` mode=`from-code` formalizes ONE change against a spec that already exists. This
      flow has no existing spec for the path yet — that is exactly the gap it fills.

    The agent does the reading itself — `bash` / `grep` / `Read` over the path and its neighbors —
    there is no dedicated recovery tool and none is being added (`AX_NO_NEW_TOOL`). It reuses the
    existing branches to write anything: `scope` for the scope spec, `module` for module specs,
    `scaffold` only if the operator wants alignment tasks. This flow owns none of that writing logic.

    Input — a path (folder or file) + the operator's intent to recover its spec. Output — the path
    lands in a confirmed scope (existing or new) with a confirmed module split, and the module specs
    for it pass the same closed-world reverse check any hand-written module spec would.
  </Mission>

  <BeliefState deps="AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE, AX_TOOL_INVOCATION, AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE, AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE, AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD, AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION, AX_OPERATOR_OUTPUT_LIVE_TEXT, AX_STATELESS_ARTIFACT, AX_SCALE_PROPORTIONAL_DEPTH, AX_READER_WITHOUT_SESSION_CONTEXT, AX_DECISION_LOG_NON_OBVIOUS">
    <Axiom id="AX_NO_NEW_TOOL">
      This flow adds no CLI command and no shared-code helper. Surveying a path — what the code does,
      who imports whom, which docs / tests sit beside it — is done live by the agent through `bash`
      (`grep`, `find`, `ls`) and `Read`, the same way any engineer would open an unfamiliar folder.
      Building a dedicated recovery tool here trades a rare, judgment-heavy task for permanent
      maintenance weight — the operator has weighed that trade and rejected it. The ONLY mechanical
      gate in this flow is the one that already exists: `gennady lint --spec=<module-spec>
      --inventory-reverse <module-dir>` (`AX_REVERSE_INVENTORY_GATE`).
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE">
      Separate Facts / Assumptions / Hypotheses when presenting the survey and the proposal.
      - **Facts** — directly read from the path: file layout, import edges, an existing test, a line
        in a README that states purpose.
      - **Assumptions** — a reasonable working read of code without a written purpose statement; mark
        explicitly, invite correction.
      - **Hypotheses** — guesses about intent that only the operator can confirm (why this exists,
        who else depends on it).

      Conflating the three lets a guess get written into the spec as if it were read from the code —
      exactly the drift `--inventory-reverse` (`AX_REVERSE_INVENTORY_GATE`) later catches on the
      inventory side, but the vision / purpose side has no mechanical check, so hygiene here is the
      only guard.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_DOSED_SURVEY">
      Read the path incrementally, cheapest signal first, and STOP once there is enough to propose a
      scope fit + a module split — do not walk every file before saying anything. Cheap-to-expensive
      order: directory listing and file names → package / workspace manifest (if any) → README / ADR
      / doc files sitting beside the path → import graph of the path's own files (who it imports, who
      imports it) → test files (what they exercise, which is often the best behavioral evidence) →
      only then, if still unclear, a fuller read of the source. Present the coarse picture before
      going deeper; the operator can always ask for more before confirming.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_REVERSE_INVENTORY_GATE">
      A recovered module spec's Entity Inventory MUST be checked against the real code with
      `gennady lint --spec=<module-spec> --inventory-reverse <module-dir>` before the flow closes.
      This is what separates recovering a spec from paraphrasing the code into narrative text: an inventory
      entry with no matching export, or an export with no inventory entry, is a finding to fix before
      closing — never a note for later.
    </Axiom>

    {{> "axiom/process/ax-artifact-style-self-check"}}
  </BeliefState>

  <KernelGrammar>
    {{> "definition/process/def-read-and-use-directive"}}
  </KernelGrammar>

  <ChatProtocol>
    {{> "contract/process/question-format"}}

    {{> "contract/process/halt-format"}}
  </ChatProtocol>

  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_PATH_NOT_FOUND` | The named path does not exist in the repo — ask the operator for the correct one |
    | `H_PATH_EMPTY` | The path exists but holds no source (empty dir, docs only) — nothing to recover FROM code |
    | `H_NO_PORTAL` | `specs/README.md` is absent — this is project-scale recovery, not one-path recovery; redirect to `ai/directives/sdd-v2/root.directive.xml` (which enters `discover-from-code` on an empty portal) |
    | `H_SCOPE_NOT_CONFIRMED` | Operator rejects or leaves open the proposed scope fit / scope-type / module split — stop; nothing is written |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Validate the path and the portal before spending any read.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Confirm the named path exists and contains source (`H_PATH_NOT_FOUND` / `H_PATH_EMPTY`
        otherwise). Confirm the portal is live (`specs/README.md` present — `H_NO_PORTAL` otherwise,
        which hands off to project-scale recovery instead). Both gates are cheap and mechanical; clear
        them before STEP_1.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_SURVEY">
      <Goal>Read the path and its neighbors — dosed, not exhaustive (`AX_DOSED_SURVEY`).</Goal>
      <Action>
        Walk the cheap-to-expensive order from `AX_DOSED_SURVEY`: layout → manifest → README / ADR /
        docs beside the path → import edges in and out → tests → deeper source read only if still
        unclear. Keep Facts / Assumptions / Hypotheses apart throughout (`AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE`). Also
        read the current portal's Scope Graph + Scopes table (`sdd-state`) — the survey needs the
        existing scopes to judge fit against.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_PROPOSE">
      <Goal>Propose scope fit, scope-type, and a module split; let the operator steer.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Present the survey plain Russian, coarse first (`AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE` tri-split, confidence
        marked per item): what the path does, which existing scope it fits OR that a new scope is
        needed, the scope-type if new, and a first-cut module split. Where the fit or the split is a
        genuine fork (new scope vs. folding into an existing one; where the module boundary falls) —
        run it through `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/interview-protocol.directive.xml")`
        so each fork gets its own question, one at a time, rather than a single multi-part ask.
        Outright rejection or an unresolved fork → `H_SCOPE_NOT_CONFIRMED`. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_RECOVER">
      <Goal>Write the spec by reusing the existing authoring branches — no bespoke writing logic here.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Before handing off, run the self-check pass per `AX_ARTIFACT_STYLE_SELF_CHECK` on any text
        this flow writes itself (the survey, the proposal); each branch called below carries its own
        copy of the same gate for the artifact it writes. On confirmation, hand off to the branches
        that already know how to write a spec, in dependency order:
        - `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/scope.directive.xml")` — create the new scope
          spec, or extend the existing one the path was folded into.
        - `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/module.directive.xml")` — write the module
          spec(s) for the confirmed split, entity inventory sourced from what STEP_1 actually read.
        - Optionally, if the operator wants the code brought in line with anything the recovery
          surfaced (missing tests, a contract gap): `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/scaffold.directive.xml")`.
        This flow contributes none of the spec-writing rules — it only routes the confirmed picture
        into the branch that owns each artifact.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_VERIFY">
      <Goal>Prove the recovered spec matches the real code, not a paraphrase of it.</Goal>
      <Action>
        For every module spec produced: `gennady lint --spec=<module-spec> --inventory-reverse
        <module-dir>` clean (`AX_REVERSE_INVENTORY_GATE`) — fix the inventory or the spec, not the
        code, when it disagrees with reality. Then `sdd-check --all .` clean over the whole tree.
        Return control.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

